        "gloss": "save"
      },
      "summary": "The malaak (messenger) gives the name and the reason for it in one breath: you will call his shamaw (name) Yashawai, because he will YaYawashayai (save) his people. The name and the promise are the same root, 𐤉𐤔𐤏 Yashai (save) — hear the name and you hear what he will do.",
      "origin": [
        {
          "ref": "Matthew 1:21",
          "marks": [
            {
              "word": "Yashawai",
              "part": "Yashawai"
            },
            {
              "word": "YaYawashayai",
              "part": "Yawashayai"
            }
          ],
          "note": "Yashawai … YaYawashayai (save): the reason for the name is the name."
        }
      ],
      "also": "Matthew 1:25; Luke 1:31; Luke 2:21",
      "common": [
        {
          "name": "Jesus",
          "where": "The New Testament of nearly every English Bible",
          "chain": [
            "Yashawai",
            "Iēsous — Greek",
            "Iesus — Latin, and the 1611 King James",
            "Jesus — English, once the J was printed"
          ],
          "sounds": "no",
          "looks": "no",
          "note": "Greek has no sh sound and no letter for 𐤏, and its men's names end in -s, so Yashawai became Iēsous; Latin wrote Iesus, and English later printed the I as a J. Nothing of Yashai (save) can be heard or seen in Jesus, so Matthew 1:21 reads as a name followed by an unrelated promise."
        },
        {
          "name": "Jeshua",
          "where": "Ezra and Nehemiah in the King James — the same 𐤉𐤔𐤅𐤏",
          "sounds": "close",
          "looks": "close",
          "note": "Keeps the sh and the ending. The J was once read as a Y, which is why it looks like the Hebrew."
        },
        {
          "name": "Joshua",
          "where": "Most English Bibles, for the longer 𐤉𐤄𐤅𐤔𐤅𐤏 Yahawashawai",
          "sounds": "partly",
          "looks": "partly",
          "note": "Keeps the sh, but the reader is never told it is the same name as Jesus.",
          "see": "yahawashawai"
        }
      ],
      "differences": [
        "One Hebrew name, three English ones. The son of Nawan (Nun) is Yahawashawai at Exodus 17:9 and Yashawai at Nehemiah 8:17 — Joshua and Jeshua in the King James — and the King James calls the same man Jesus at Acts 7:45 and Hebrews 4:8, because in Greek both forms are one name, Iēsous.",
        "In the King James, Matthew 1:21 puts Jesus beside save; the reader has to be told they are the same word. In the Hebrew they cannot be missed: Yashawai … YaYawashayai.",
        "Changing the name kept the person but dropped the meaning. Reading Yashawai keeps both."
      ],
      "compare": "Exodus 17:9; Nehemiah 8:17; Acts 7:45; Hebrews 4:8",
      "related": [
        "yahawashawai"
      ],
      "english": "Jesus"
    },
